/**
 * The hero on /products/api-hosting: butterflies flying under the words, one for each server framework, seen through
 * the glass that /products/agent looks through.
 *
 * Three things are drawn, all of them out of the same fine, grainy lines as /products/sdk-generator:
 *
 * 1. The land. Under the words lies a landscape after a watercolour, in hairlines: water along the top,
 *    hills and dark clumps of trees below it, a sandy meadow thinning to specks at the foot. Each line is a
 *    place across a sheet, so the fragment shader can draw any number of them for the price of one mesh.
 * 2. The butterflies. Each follows the land's flow on real wings, in 3D, seen from behind and a little above, with its
 *    wings going from nearly flat to nearly shut. The wings are glass in pastel yellow, orange or blue: pale where the light comes through at the root,
 *    deepening outwards, with fine strokes fanning through them and a soft dab of warm colour where they gather.
 *    They beat in bursts, climbing in hops, and glide down between them. Nothing is painted on a wing, and they carry
 *    nothing. (Each used to carry a little planet on a thread with its framework's logo on it; the mesh no longer has
 *    the thread or the planet, so the code that swung and printed them below is not drawn.)
 * 3. Their wakes. Where a butterfly has just been, a few lines of the air it flies in show its colour, and
 *    let it go.
 *
 * The land and the wakes stay crisp all the way to the edge. Only the butterflies go through the glass: they
 * are drawn into a texture of their own, and that goes to the screen through one lens, over the land. Nothing
 * is drawn differently near the edge of the window: towards it the lens magnifies more and more, and bends
 * red, green and blue by slightly different amounts, so a butterfly leaving the window comes apart into glass.
 * The lens is the one on /products/agent. It runs up both sides here as well as along the top, since the
 * butterflies are going across the window and out at the sides.
 *
 * Pages on scalar.com render on the client and hydrate over the first copy of the hero, so binding goes
 * through a MutationObserver with a dataset flag on the element: a node replaced by hydration comes back
 * unbound and heals itself.
 */
